  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "A particle moves from the origin with an acceleration of $(-i+3j)\\,{\\text{cm/s}}^{2}$ and an initial velocity of $(4i+j)\\,\\text{cm/s}.$ Find the position vector of the particle after $2$ seconds.",
  "worked_solution": "If a particle moves with constant acceleration $a,$ the position vector of the particle at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nr={r}_{0}+ut+\\frac{1}{2}a{t}^{2},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${r}_{0}$ is the initial position of the particle and $u$ is its initial velocity.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{0}=0i+0j,\\,u=4i+j,\\,a=-i+3j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position vector of the particle after $t=2\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =(0i+0j)+(4i+j)(2)+\\frac{1}{2}(-i+3j)(2)^{2} \\\\  & =(8i+2j)+2(-i+3j) \\\\  & =(8i+2j)+(-2i+6j) \\\\  & =(6i+8j)\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
